Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): How do we know that information is truthful when you present a driver's license as proof of identity? How do we trust that the data on. It is correct. The answer is provenance. Provenance is the record of where data came from, how it was

61
00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:26.359
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Created and how it changed over time. It helps us answer critical questions. Who created the data? Has it been altered? Can we trust it.

62
00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:54.539
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Let's introduce 2 generalized design patterns for tracking data provenance. The 1st approach follows data as it is updated using logs to track. Who made what changes and when this is how many traditional databases work, there's a central record, and every modification is noted in an audit log. If a mistake is made, we can check the logs and roll back to a previous version. This works well when a trusted authority maintains the logs, but it relies on the governance and security of that authority.

63
00:04:54.699 --> 00:05:19.209
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): The second approach treats data as immutable record. Instead of updating the same entry, each change is recorded as a new block of information which references the previous block creating a chain of records. This ledger-based system, similar to how blockchains work, ensures that past records cannot be altered without detection. The entire history of the data is preserved creating a potentially transparent and tamper-resistant system.

64
00:05:19.219 --> 00:05:42.089
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Both methods serve different needs. Log-based tracking is efficient and commonly used in centralized systems. Ledger-based tracking ensures stronger integrity and is ideal for decentralized environments, enabling high trust at its core. Provenance is about trust. It allows us to verify not just what information says, but how it got there, giving us confidence in the truth behind the data

