00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:29.290
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Administrative trust is the foundation of how we manage identity and data. Today, it means trusting a centralized authority like a bank, a government agency, or a company to maintain and verify our information on our behalf. This model is efficient and widely used. When you log into a website, reset a password or prove your identity, you're relying on administrative trust.

84
00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:55.129
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): These organizations ensure security by using policies, audits, and internal controls. They have logs of who accessed what and when they restrict access to sensitive information, and they follow industry standards to prevent security breaches. But administrative trust has its limits. It's centralized meaning that if an authority is compromised through hacking mismanagement or even just a technical failure, your data and identity could be at risk.

85
00:07:55.130 --> 00:08:20.640
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): And because these systems are controlled by individual organizations, they don't always scale well, each company has its own login system, its own rules and its own way of managing access which can create inefficiencies and security gaps. Ultimately administrative trust works, because we believe these institutions will do their job correctly. But what if we had a way to establish trust without relying on central authority? That's where cryptographic trust comes in.

